A||||5||Phil||Speaker Bridging???||||1||
Z||000000||Phil||08-10-2000||08:19 AM||||Dear Audio Gurus:<BR>My son has a prob with his speakers. The new radio he got(Jensen 715K) doesn't like the 10 ohm front and 4 ohm rear factory setup as it keep blowing some transistor in the unit. The repair shop said something about bridging the speakers to knock the fronts down to below 8 ohms. Anybody know what this means? His money is tight so he would rather not have to go for new ones right now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-10-2000||08:52 AM||||just spend a couple buck and get the 4ohm units that replace them.. OR you can got to readio shack and spent a few bucks less and get a coil to change the resistance. don't drop below 4ohms or you'll still have the same problem. i wouldn't bridge anything cause you'll have a muddy sounding system. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||08-10-2000||10:28 AM||||the only other way you could do it is by adding speakers to the circuit.  or using ALOT of speaker wire (like 10 miles)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||08-10-2000||11:05 AM||||maybe try this. go to radio shack and get a pair of those really small surface mount speakers. i think they're 3 or 4" speakers that are on sale from time to time and fairly cheap any way. you take a screw out of the trim around the door in the dead pedal area. the speakers are small enough that the one screw will hold the bracket that holds the speaker. you may have to get a longer screw of the same type, i don't remember if i did.<P>this will let you do a few things. you can run them in parallel with the dash speakers for about 7 ohms impedance, or run them on their own at 4 ohms, probaly with a bit of loss on the low end though.  also this lets you try out the location for permanent speaker pods. then you pull those out and use them the same way in other cars for experimental speakers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||olympic||08-10-2000||01:38 PM||||OK, this doesn't make any sense.  A speaker with too much resistance should not hurt your deck.  Remember, a speaker rated at 4 ohms is not always 4 ohms.  Resistance changes with frequency and it is not uncommon to see readings of 30 ohms or higher at certain frequencies.  So 10 ohms shouldn't cause a problem.<BR>    Does your deck have 4 speaker outputs and are you using one output for each of the 4 speakers?  If your deck only has 2 speaker outputs and you are paralleling 2 speakers on each output it will cause a problem.  4 ohm parallelled with 10 ohms = 2.8 ohms.<P>  Get an ohm meter and check the DC resistance in each speaker.  Maybe one is shorted out or something.  The DC resistance should be close to its rated resistance 4 or 10 ohms. Good luck!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||08-10-2000||02:00 PM||||Supposedly it is the imbalance from front to rear that was causing the prob. The head unit works it just won't shut off properly with the key. We have come up with a stop gap solution until his $$ situation changes. He is going to put an old set of the 4 ohm 4x6 rears that we have laying around into the front.<BR>Thanks to all who offered their input||||reg||1
